 cold medicine example suppose I  give some samples of cold medicine to my  coughing friends and you give some to  your coughing friends and we record how  many of them stop coughing within the 48  hour period we've collected data now  what well do you think we're ready to  move on  not quite you see what we've just  introduced is a bias and we put that  into our data it's likely that our close  friends have similar characteristics as  we do maybe they're in our same age  bracket or they live in the same city in  my case most of them are women so in  order for our results to be widely  applicable we have to collect data in a  way that's objective and rigorous one  improvement we could stand in front of a  drugstore and sample people as they go  in and out another perhaps might be to  call random numbers from a phone book  and ask people to participate in our  trial but both of these methods are  still biased if we're sampling only from  a particular group then our results will  only apply to that group if we want to  remove bias entirely we have to collect  a sample where every person has an equal  chance of being selected now once we've  stated the question and collected  appropriate data we can move to step  three which is analyze the data this is  where we really begin to see the ideas  of probability and statistics take shape  literally statisticians often start an  analysis with a graphical look at the  data to get an overall picture of it we  use plots graphs tables anything that  can help us reveal patterns or  relationships within our data  now a plot or a graph can be a more  effective way of presenting data than  just a massive numbers because we can  see where the data is clustered and  where there's only a few data values  then we could also use formal techniques  like hypothesis testing or linear  regression to determine if or how much  our data supports our initial claim  ultimately we want to use our analysis  to make a decision about our problem  statement which leads us to step four in  the last step we draw a conclusion here  we consider all the information that  we've gathered and we analyzed and we  make a definitive statement about our  problem something like study shows that  numerical cough medicine relieves  coughing in 48 hours guaranteed how's  that sound  all right well that conclusion is too  strong in fact a conclusion with zero  uncertainty is really too good to be  true but we do want to extend the  results from our sample data the data  that we collected to the overall  population more generally this lesson is  going to give you a strong foundation in  probablistic reasoning and the basic  tools needed to make sense of it all we  first start with sampling which refers  to selecting at random one or more of  all possible outcomes so for example  selecting a random sample of Americans  for a political poll or maybe even  drawing a hand of 5 cards out of a deck  of 52 or finding 10 pennies on the  sidewalk and recording their ages  sampling helps us draw inference about  the larger population of outcomes now  drawing random samples forms the  foundation of probability which is all  about considering the possible outcomes  and enix of an experiment and  quantifying their chances how many of us  have ever heard a weather forecaster  declare there's a 75 percent chance of  rain today well what this really means  is that in a large number of days with  atmospheric conditions like those today  the proportion  days in which rain occurs is 75% so why  then does probability refer to the  long-run well it's simply because we  can't accurately determine a probability  with a small number of observations if I  sample 10 people and they're all  right-handed I can't conclude that  probability of being right-handed is a  hundred percent it takes a much larger  sample of people to accurately predict  the proportion of people that are truly  right-handed so what happens when we  have real but limited data and we want  to make a conclusion from it maybe  calculate some probabilities we don't  have time to go in the long run so what  do we do with this limited sample size  or here's an example in that case  suppose we wanted to understand how a  grocery store displays their cereal  boxes and whether different types of  cereals are targeted to adults or  children we go into a grocery store and  we notice that there are six rows of  shelves and each shelf has five boxes of  cereal we can group the six shelves into  three categories the bottom two shelves  the middle two shelves and the top two  shelves here's a look at that data now  notice in the first column we have  adults children and the total the second  column tells us how many boxes of cereal  are located on the top two shelves so  nine boxes are targeted at adults one  for children for a total of ten boxes  likewise in the middle two shelves three  boxes of cereals are targeted at adults  seven for children and a total of ten  and then you see the bottom two no boxes  targeted at adults 10 for children out  of a total of ten the far most right  column total is going to add up the  total number of boxes that are targeted  at adults which is twelve and targeted  at children which is 18 suppose we  wanted to know the probability that  breakfast cereal is targeted at adults  let's call this event a well the  probability of a would be 12  which is the total number targeted at  adults out of 30 which is the total  number of cereal boxes or 40% now we can  do the same type of calculation and  determine the probability of breakfast  cereal being in the middle two rows for  instance let's call that B the  probability of B would be 10 out of 30  or 0.33 now is natural to think about  how to events relate to each other for  example what's the probability that the  cereal is targeted at adults and located  on the middle two shelves now in this  case we want to look at our data to see  where those two events occur together  notice t